frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Vibraphone C4, pilot: a bar of a builder's F3-F6 vibraphone design in EN AW-2017A aluminium over the builder's as-built resonator tube, its undercut solved in the engine to the tuning rule, a marimba mallet as a declared stand-in. The bar's own radiation damping joins its loss (bar_self_radiation on), over the near-vacuum loss row.
name	vibraphone-c4-wyndorps-2017a-rubber-tube
source	Bar outline: the Wyndorps DIY vibraphone pages, www.wyndorps.de, page "Macro Geometrie", bar-set drawing (media/images/plattensatz.jpg, read twice at two image sizes, both reads agree): C4 bar 323 mm long and 57.15 mm (2 1/4 in) wide, the drawing's labels in whole millimetres (read uncertainty 0.5 mm). Builder design data for an instrument on an M55-type frame with bars cut to the American lengths of an F3-F6 set, not a maker's spec sheet. Thickness 12.0 mm on every bar, the low end of the builder's measured 12.0 to 12.33 mm (no per-bar value printed). Alloy EN AW-2017A as the builder names it: E 72500 MPa, G 27200 MPa, nu 0.33 and density 2790 kg/m3 from the Euralliage 2017A sheet, through the pack named below. Tube: the builder's resonator-unit CAD (media/files/resonanzrohreinheit.zip, PETG parts and parts list, "M55 resonance tube unit") and the builder's frame assembly with the tubes and bar posts in place (page "Suitcase Frame", media/files/3d-m55.zip, HBG_0000237088_asm.stp): parts C4-upper and C4-lower, glued, 306.0 mm long outside; the air column is 304.0 mm, from the mouth to the floor face of C4-lower, a cup with a 2.0 mm floor.
note	NOT in the source: the undercut. No undercut is printed, so the arc is solved in the engine, modes only, to put f1 at equal temperament on A4 = 442 Hz with f2 = 4 f1, the tuning rule a maker uses (442 Hz is the tuning the maker prints for its M55-type vibraphones, "Tuning: 442 Hertz" on the Musser M55 page: a current specification, the recording's instrument and year are not named, and when any such instrument moved from 440 is not verified); the solve starts from the solved 4:1 cut of the marimba C4 card, its half reach and middle thickness carried as fractions of that bar's length and thickness. The shape comes from the tuning rule, not from a measured bar. The solved cut: half reach 90.995 mm, middle 5.106 mm, giving f1 262.815 Hz (+0.001 c from 262.815 Hz), f2/f1 4.00001 and f3/f1 9.21921. The tube's bore and air column are read from the CAD parts: radius 30.0 mm, C4-upper's inner cylinder (a 1.5 mm wall), and air column 304 mm (outside 306, 2.0 mm cup floor); the card first carried 306 mm, the outside length. The tube is taken as built, so its stop is not re-solved; alone, its first resonance is 265.4 Hz against the note's 262.8 Hz. Not modelled: C4-lower's joint ring, which fits inside C4-upper, narrows the bore 48 to 60 mm above the floor face, down to radius 27.9 mm over its middle 5.5 mm; a lossless plane-wave transfer matrix over the CAD profile puts it at +0.92 Hz on the tube's first mode, which the engine's uniform bore leaves out. The bar-to-tube gap is not printed (searched: US 5977465, US 2008/0105105, US 3138986, a 2022 conference paper on bar-resonator coupling, the builder's pages, frame instructions, photos, two of the builder's videos and both of the builder's frame CAD sets), so it is 12.7 mm, the xylophone cards' stand-in, the 1/2 in end of Flynt's printed 1/4 to 1/2 in, below the plane of the uncut lower face. The builder's frame assembly bounds it: at all 91 bar posts the mouth plane is 18.00 mm below the centre of the posts' cord holes and slots, so the gap is 18.00 mm less the cord centre's height above the bar's lower face, a height not printed. With the cord anywhere 4 to 9 mm up the 12.0 mm bar, the gap is 9 to 14 mm; with the bar's hole at mid-thickness, 4 to 5 mm across (not printed), and the bar resting on the 3 mm cord the builder names, it is 11.0 to 11.5 mm. On the 306 mm column first carried, f1 decayed at 7.59 dB/s with the gap at 6.35 mm (1/4 in) against 7.05 at 12.7 mm; no gap is taken from that spread. Mallet: the marimba cards' rubber-mallet rows (Chaigne and Doutaut 1997, rubber on padouk), a declared stand-in on aluminium; vibraphone mallets are usually cord- or yarn-wrapped, and the recording's mallet is unstated. Strike fraction, receiver, band, windows and mesh carried from the marimba card of the same note. Fan valves: not modelled; the recording's notes carry no envelope modulation at one rate common to all of them, so its motor was off. Not the recording's instrument: Iowa's vibraphone (sustain mf, C4-B4) names no model and its sets run C3 to F6, while this bar and tube come from a builder's F3-F6 design. The tube is a closed-open column of five odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 60 mm across inside, is wider than this bar. Loss: the loss factor 8.340e-5 on every bar mode, Howell's clean 2024-T4 aluminium beam 56 at 5.6e-4 atm (NASA TM X-2852, 1974, Table III), NOT a 2017A bar and NOT a vibraphone bar, measured with the air pumped out. The bar's own radiation comes back from the engine (bar_self_radiation on): each band mode's radiated power from the same Burton-Miller solve that gives the receiver, W per unit modal velocity, its amplitude decay rate in 1/s, added to the loss as max(W, 0). A negative W is below the boundary-element resolution and adds nothing; the most negative W on this bar sets the floor, about 5e-3 per second, and the render's record prints every W and the floor. At the bar's third vertical mode, 2422.9 Hz, W is about 4.3 per second, some 900 times the floor; at f1 W comes out negative and adds nothing, so f1 keeps the near-vacuum loss alone, 0.60 dB/s for the bar by itself. Viscous air loss is declared out, not modelled. The recording's f1, with its tube on, decays at 2.77 to 3.25 dB/s (T60 18 to 22 s, 3 strikes). Not modelled: the support cords and felts, and the mallet's contact loss; nothing is adjusted to close the gap.
